===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰedʰ-}}
{{bor+|en|fr|abattoir}}, from {{m|fr|abattre||to slaughter}} (cognate to {{m|en|abate}}) + {{m|fr|-oir||-ory}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.əˌtwɑː(ɹ)/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.əˌtwɑɹ/|/ˈæb.əˌtwɑ/|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-abattoir.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-ca-abattoir.ogg|a=CA}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-abattoir.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{hyph|en|ab|at|toir}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A public [[slaughterhouse]] for [[cattle]], [[sheep]], etc. {{defdate|Early 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref>{{R:SOED5|page=2}}</ref>
#: {{ux|en|Once the cows reach maturity, they're sent to the '''abattoir'''.}}
# A place or event [[liken]]ed to a slaughterhouse, because of great [[carnage]] or [[bloodshed]].<ref>American Heritage Dictionary of the English Language, Fourth Edition</ref>
#: {{syn|en|bloodbath}}
#: {{ux|en|The army's raid on the enemy turned into a major '''abattoir'''.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2017-04-19|author=Franz-Stefan Gady|title=What Would the Second Korean War Look Like?|work=w:The Diplomat|url=https://thediplomat.com/2017/04/what-would-the-second-korean-war-look-like/|passage=The corridors where North Korean troops would be advancing [in a hypothetical invasion of South Korea] would almost certainly be turned into human '''abattoirs'''.}}

